meta {
  name: Request catalog (as consumer)
  type: http
  seq: 16
}

post {
  url: {{mgmtUrl}}/v1/catalog/request
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
}

body:json {
  {
    "@context": ["https://w3id.org/edc/connector/management/v2"],
    "@type": "CatalogRequest",
    "counterPartyAddress": "{{dsp}}",
    "counterPartyId": "{{counterPartyId}}",
    "protocol": "dataspace-protocol-http:2025-1"
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
  res.body["@type"]: eq Catalog
}

docs {
  Use the "two-connectors" environment (docs/TESTING.md, Two connectors).
  This is the consumer's management API, not the DSP endpoint POST /catalog/request:
  the connector gets a DCP token from its own STS, asks the provider and returns the
  provider's Catalog as it came. Run it before "Start negotiation": the offer to
  negotiate is in a dataset's hasPolicy.
  The body is the CatalogRequest of Eclipse EDC's management API v4. protocol (or
  profile) must be dataspace-protocol-http:2025-1; a querySpec gets 400; additionalScopes
  are added to the token. @context and @type may be left out.
  502 when the provider refuses, does not answer or sends more than 16 MiB; the detail
  says which.
}
